{
  "summary": "The app fails the most critical part of the user journey – accepting the policy. The custom checkbox on PolicyScreen is missing required ARIA attributes (aria‑checked) and does not toggle its state, which leaves the Continue button permanently disabled and breaks the primary flow. Axe reports a critical violation for the same element. Several other accessibility and design‑token compliance issues were spotted (missing accessibility labels on navigation tabs, inconsistent use of theme spacing, missing autocomplete on form fields, etc.). These issues are mostly medium‑to‑low severity, but the policy‑checkbox bug must be fixed before any other quality improvements can be considered.",
  "findings": [
    {
      "severity": "high",
      "file": "src/screens/PolicyScreen.tsx",
      "issue": "The custom checkbox is rendered as a <div> with role=\"checkbox\" and aria-label but without the required aria-checked attribute, and its internal state never updates on click. This prevents the user from checking the box and enables the Continue button, breaking the primary onboarding flow.",
      "fix": "Replace the custom element with React Native Paper's <Checkbox> (or a native <input type=\"checkbox\">) and bind its checked state to a React state variable. Add aria-checked={checked} (or let the component handle it) and ensure the onPress toggles the state. Update the Continue button’s disabled prop based on the checked state."
    },
    {
      "severity": "high",
      "file": "src/components/ui/BottomTabNavigator.tsx",
      "issue": "Bottom navigation tabs are missing accessibilityLabel and explicit role=\"navigation\". On web, focus‑visible styling is not applied, and the touch target may be smaller than the required 44 × 44 px.",
      "fix": "Add accessibilityLabel to each tab (e.g., \"Dashboard tab\"), set role=\"navigation\" on the container, and ensure the hit area meets the 44 px minimum (use invisible padding if needed). Add CSS for :focus-visible to show a high‑contrast outline."
    },
    {
      "severity": "medium",
      "file": "src/components/ui/FormField.tsx",
      "issue": "TextInput components lack appropriate autoComplete attributes and use generic keyboardType values, which reduces form ergonomics and keyboard accessibility.",
      "fix": "Pass autoComplete props that match the field purpose (e.g., autoComplete=\"email\" for email, \"off\" for amount if needed) and set the correct keyboardType (numeric for amount, date for date fields)."
    },
    {
      "severity": "medium",
      "file": "src/components/ui/PrimaryButton.tsx",
      "issue": "When loading, the button label is replaced solely by a spinner, losing the accessible text label. The button also does not expose accessibilityState.busy.",
      "fix": "Keep the button label visible when loading (e.g., \"Saving receipt…\") and add accessibilityState={{ busy: true }} so screen readers announce the busy state."
    },
    {
      "severity": "medium",
      "file": "src/screens/DashboardScreen.tsx",
      "issue": "The screen wrapper does not include an explicit landmark (accessibilityRole=\"main\") and the headline is not marked as a header, violating heading hierarchy expectations.",
      "fix": "Wrap the screen content in the shared <Screen> component which should set accessibilityRole=\"main\" and render the first headline with accessibilityRole=\"header\". Verify the hierarchy (h1 then h2/h3)."
    },
    {
      "severity": "low",
      "file": "src/theme.ts",
      "issue": "Several components (e.g., SummaryCard, EmptyState) use hard‑coded spacing values like marginBottom: 16px and borderRadius: 8px instead of the defined theme.spacing and theme.roundness tokens.",
      "fix": "Replace literal numbers with theme.spacing.sm / theme.roundness wherever possible to ensure consistency and easier design updates."
    },
    {
      "severity": "low",
      "file": "src/components/ui/EmptyState.tsx",
      "issue": "Class names are applied (e.g., \"emptyState\") without corresponding CSS rules, leading to unstyled elements if the CSS is missing.",
      "fix": "Either define the required CSS classes in a stylesheet or remove the classNames and rely solely on the style prop (or styled-components) to apply layout."
    },
    {
      "severity": "low",
      "file": "src/screens/SettingsScreen.tsx",
      "issue": "The Delete confirmation dialog uses a plain TextInput for the DELETE keyword without a visible label, making it hard for screen readers to convey purpose.",
      "fix": "Add a label prop to the TextInput (e.g., label=\"Type DELETE to confirm\") and link error/helper text via accessibilityHint."
    }
  ]
}

